INT. PENCIL FACTORY - OPEN-PLAN OFFICE - DAY

A mezzanine office above the production floor. Sawdust sifts through the floor grating. Fifty identical desks, each with a cup of sharpened pencils. A banner overhead reads: "WE'RE ALL POINT PEOPLE."

The air smells of cedar and stale coffee.

AUGUSTA MALOUF-PRIOR (50s, pearls, reading glasses on a chain) sits at the head of the room under a framed poster of a pencil. Her desk is clear except for a single yellow No. 2.

At the next desk, QUENTIN BOWDEN-AKINTOLA (30s, short-sleeved dress shirt, tie with tiny pencils on it) types with two fingers. Across the aisle, LORETTA GRUNDY (60s, cardigan, thermos shaped like a pencil) reads a printout without moving her lips. At the back, in a corner no one has assigned, sits SIMEON PELL (20s, headphones around his neck, pencil behind each ear), the only person who has ever seen the factory floor in person.

AUGUSTA
Team, a quick update.

Nobody looks up. She taps the pencil against the desk.

AUGUSTA (CONT'D)
Management has decided to eliminate the red pencils.

QUENTIN
All of them?

AUGUSTA
All of them, Quentin.

LORETTA
Augusta, we've sold red pencils since 1952.

AUGUSTA
And we'll continue selling them until the stock runs out.

SIMEON
So we don't make them anymore.

AUGUSTA
Simeon, we make them differently.

SIMEON
How differently?

AUGUSTA
Without the red.

A silence. Sawdust drifts. Loretta turns a page.

QUENTIN
Does that mean they're no longer red pencils?

AUGUSTA
It means they're pencils.

LORETTA
(muttering)
They were always pencils.

AUGUSTA
Loretta, this is an exciting transition.

LORETTA
Augusta, I've seen four transitions. They all ended in lunch.

Simeon lifts a pencil from behind his ear and turns it in his fingers. He looks at the tip. Quentin resumes typing.

SIMEON
Do the blue ones stay?

AUGUSTA
The blue ones are under review.

SIMEON
And the green?

AUGUSTA
We don't discuss green.

QUENTIN
(whisper)
What happened to green?

LORETTA
(whisper)
Don't ask.

The banner overhead flaps in a draft from the floor. Augusta sets the pencil down in the exact center of her desk, aligns it with the edge, and smiles at the room as though it had applauded.

AUGUSTA
Wonderful. Let's circle back after lunch.
